1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi. In this video, we'll look at how we can use Burp to identify reflected input.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Reflected input is when data is copied from a request and echoed into the application's immediate response.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
This is a prerequisite for a range of vulnerabilities, including reflected cross-site scripting.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
You can use Burp to test for reflected input by either using Burp Scanner to automatically flag reflected input,

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
or by using Burp Repeater to manually identify it.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:44,000
If you'd like to follow along with the steps, you can use the reflected XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded lab from our Web Security Academy.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
First, we'll look at using Burp Scanner.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Once the lab has launched, identify a request you want to investigate.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Right-click and choose Do Active Scan.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
You can now go to Burp's dashboard and review the Issue Activity panel on the dashboard or on the specific scan task

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
to identify any reflected input that Burp Scanner has flagged.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Next, we'll look at identifying reflected input manually.

13
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
In the Proxy HTTP History tab, right-click the message you want to investigate and select Send to Repeater.

14
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Replace the value of a parameter with an easily identifiable unique string.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
For example, XSS Test.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Click Send.

17
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Review the response to see if it contains the unique string.

18
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
You can use the Text Editor Search function to easily identify whether the unique string is reflected in the response.

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
You can now repeat this process for each parameter in the request.

20
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Once you have identified reflected input, you can investigate the request further to test for vulnerabilities such as

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Cross-site scripting, Client-side template injection, Server-side template injection, and Input transformations.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
You can now repeat the request for the link to see if it contains the link in the description.

23
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

24
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

25
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

27
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

28
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

29
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

30
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

32
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

33
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

34
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
You can now repeat the link in the description.

